Specifically, the elastic modulus and ultimate compressive strength obtained from the 90-day compression tests of straw-mixed lime mortar were incorporated into the constitutive model as defined in the relevant standards. Additionally, the uniaxial compressive damage evolution parameters were modified to accelerate the post-peak stress reduction. Given the difficulty of fabricating and testing tensile specimens, the ultimate tensile strength was approximated from the tensile stress at the mid-span of the beam at the onset of cracking, as derived from flexural tests. Since the experimental failure mode resembled that of concrete, and our analysis focused primarily on the pre-cracking response rather than post-failure behavior, the modified concrete damage plasticity model was adopted to simulate the mechanical behavior of straw-mixed lime mortar. To account for the characteristic rapid post-peak stress decline, the descending branch of the constitutive law was adjusted accordingly. The peak compressive stress was set to 0.5 MPa, and the elastic modulus to 16 MPa. 4 d c = 1 - ρ c n n - 1 + x n ( x ≤ 1 ) 1 - ρ c α c ( x - 1 ) 2 + x ( x > 1 ) \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
